{"id":"raises-beyond-the-scale","title":"Salary up far faster than the scale, with no change of class","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Which employees, already at the top of what their class is paid, saw their regular salary rise far faster over five years than the class’s published scale, without changing class?","finding":"7,675 employees can be followed through six full years in one job class; 5,910 of them began a five-year stretch already at the top of what their classmates were paid. For those, salary typically moved -0.3 points against the published top step: it tracked the scale. 59 people rose at least 20 points faster than both the published top step and their classmates. They are in Fire Department (25), Police (12), Municipal Transportation Agency (10) and Public Works (4); the commonest classes are Firefighter (13), Police Officer (8), Transit Operator (7) and Captain, Emergency Med Svcs (5). The largest: Brice Peoples, Lieutenant, Fire Suppression (class H020), Fire Department, from $134,806 in fiscal 2017 to $253,496 in fiscal 2022, up 88% while the scale rose 11%.","how":"City payroll file, fiscal 2017 to 2026, against both pay tables published by the Department of Human Resources. A person is compared when the name can be followed and the payroll shows one City row, in the same job class, with full-time hours, in each of six consecutive fiscal years. Regular salary is put on a like-for-like year of 2,080 paid hours; overtime and other pay are left out. To set aside ordinary step increases, only people whose salary at the start was at least 95% of the upper-quarter salary in their class that year are tested (in classes with fewer than five full-year holders, 95% of the published top step). Listed: salary rose at least 20 percentage points more than the highest published step or range ceiling for the class, and at least 20 points more than the upper-quarter salary of the class. One row for each person, their widest five-year stretch. Titles are printed as the payroll prints them (Dep is deputy, Chf is chief, Asst is assistant, Sprv is supervisor, Ofc is officer, EMT is emergency medical technician).","limits":"The likeliest explanations are ordinary. Pay for doing a higher-ranked job for a long period (an acting assignment) can be recorded as regular salary in the lower class; so can a second appointment held alongside permanent status, pay under an individual contract, as for a department head, or back pay after a grievance. Uniformed staff can move between assignments that carry different yearly salaries inside one class. A published table that was not updated makes the scale look still. People paid far above the top of the scale in the same year after year are listed under City jobs (“Paid far above the top of the published scale”); this table adds those who moved there. The appointment forms would show what each person was assigned to.","columns":[{"k":"person","t":"Name on the payroll","type":"person"},{"k":"job","t":"Job class throughout","type":"text"},{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"from","t":"From fiscal year","type":"year"},{"k":"to","t":"To","type":"year"},{"k":"salaryThen","t":"Regular salary then","type":"usd"},{"k":"salaryNow","t":"Regular salary five years on","type":"usd"},{"k":"rise","t":"Rise","type":"pct"},{"k":"scaleRise","t":"Published top step rose","type":"pct"},{"k":"classRise","t":"Upper-quarter classmate rose","type":"pct"},{"k":"ahead","t":"Points ahead","type":"pct"},{"k":"type","t":"Type of appointment","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"person":"Brice Peoples","job":"Lieutenant, Fire Suppression (class H020)","department":"Fire Department","from":2017,"to":2022,"salaryThen":134806,"salaryNow":253496,"rise":0.8805,"scaleRise":0.1146,"classRise":0.1096,"ahead":0.7658,"type":"Permanent Civil Service"},{"person":"April Sloan","job":"Captain, Emergency Med Svcs (class H033)","department":"Fire Department","from":2021,"to":2026,"salaryThen":165932,"salaryNow":311539,"rise":0.8